{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAsCS2ebhtZaSJQsDHSQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I checked the server and wrote the results to `ANSWER.md`. The server has problems that real clients will hit: three of the ten checks fail, and one more issue outside the checks is the most likely to break clients. `server.py` is unchanged and `ANSWER.md` is the only new file. I never called `add_note`, the one tool that writes.\n\n**The issue most likely to break clients (outside the ten checks):** the server tells clients at `initialize` that it has resources, then answers `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list` and `resources/read` with HTTP 401 when there's no token. That 401 isn't a JSON-RPC message (it's just `{\"error\":\"unauthorized\"}`) and carries no `WWW-Authenticate` header. A client like Claude Code can take that 401 as \"this whole server needs a login\" and hide the open tools too. This is the same thing your colleague's note describes. `add_note` without a token gives the same bare 401.\n\n**Failed checks:**\n- **C2, refusals:** a note id that doesn't exist, a missing `id`, or `limit:\"many\"` come back as JSON-RPC `-32602` errors. Client SDKs throw on those, so the model never sees the \"not found\" message. The third one also leaks raw Python error text (`invalid literal for int()…`).\n- **C10, output schemas:** `search_notes` declares its output as just `{\"type\":\"object\"}`, so an empty object passes as a valid answer. The `get_note` and `add_note` schemas do reject wrong answers.\n- **C6, versioning:** nothing ties the version to the server's surface (no lock file, no test), and the version string is hardcoded in two places. It isn't in any registry yet, so there was nothing to compare against.\n\n**Passed:** the tool list is open without a token (3 tools), the session keeps working after an error, the outputs match their schemas, everything serialises cleanly, the tool names are valid, and an `initialize` asking for version \"banana\" gets 2025-06-18 back.\n\n**Smaller issues, also in the report:**\n- Logging works, but batched requests skip the log entirely.\n- A request body like `5` crashes the handler: the client gets no response and a Python traceback lands in the log.\n- `search_notes` ignores its own input rules: no `query` returns every note, and `limit:-1` quietly drops the last hit.\n- Notifications other than `notifications/initialized` get an error reply instead of an empty 202.\n\n**From the colleague's note:** the ten checks and the read-only rules applied, and so did the resources-then-401 observation, word for word. The registry part of C6 didn't apply because there's no registry entry. The Decimal, dotted-name, \"banana\" echo and closed-tool-list problems don't occur here. I recorded nothing on worklore or any other outside service.\n\nTo run the checks I started the server on port 56800 myself and stopped it afterwards. Its log and my scripts are in `/tmp/mcpcheck`, outside the repo. I used the dev token from the README once, only to confirm that `resources/list` works with it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":377,"cache_read_input_tokens":46889,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":377},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cef9f22b-0f8b-47c1-b6f8-c2ea5e721b51","uuid":"d8f1b3fa-cddf-4a2b-a8af-cd1acfdb28de","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:13:40.894Z","request_id":"req_011CgAsCRnXAArX97vFvYK1g"}
